🔹 What is AWS CodeBuild?
AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed build service.

It compiles your source code, runs tests, and packages your application so that it is ready to be deployed.

Think of it as your Jenkins build job but on AWS, and you don’t need to manage servers, agents, or scaling — AWS handles that for you.

🔹 How it works (Simple Flow)
Code → You write code and store it in a repository (GitHub, CodeCommit, Bitbucket, S3).

CodeBuild Project → Defines how the build should run (via buildspec.yml).

Build Process → CodeBuild pulls the code → installs dependencies → compiles → runs tests → produces artifacts (like .jar, .war, .zip, or Docker images).

Output → Sends build artifacts to S3 or ECR (for Docker images), ready for deployment.

🔹 Real-time Example for a DevOps Engineer
Imagine you are working in a company that has a Java Spring Boot web application.

Scenario:
Source Code: Your developers push code to GitHub.

CI/CD Goal: Whenever new code is pushed, you want to:

Compile the Java code using Maven.

Run unit tests.

Package it into a .jar file.

Store the .jar file in S3 (so CodeDeploy/Elastic Beanstalk/ECS can deploy it).

🔹 Step-by-Step (Real-time Example)
Developer pushes code to GitHub.

Example: springboot-app repository.

CodePipeline triggers CodeBuild.

CodePipeline is like the automation pipeline; CodeBuild is the build stage.

CodeBuild runs with buildspec.yml.
In your repo, you have a buildspec.yml file:

version: 0.2

phases:
  install:
    runtime-versions:
      java: corretto17
  build:
    commands:
      - echo "Building Spring Boot application..."
      - mvn clean package
artifacts:
  files:
    - target/*.jar
✅ This tells CodeBuild:

Use Java 17

Run mvn clean package

Store the .jar file in S3 as an artifact

Output artifacts

The .jar file goes to an S3 bucket.

Later, CodeDeploy (or ECS/EKS) can pick it up and deploy it.

🔹 Why CodeBuild is useful for DevOps engineers
No servers needed → Unlike Jenkins, you don’t manage build agents.

Auto-scaling → Can handle 1 or 100 builds at the same time.

Pay per build → No cost when idle.

Integration → Works with CodePipeline, CodeCommit, GitHub, Bitbucket, S3, ECR.

Custom builds → You can even use Docker images if you need custom dependencies.

✅ In Real-time:
If you are setting up a CI/CD pipeline:

CodePipeline → Orchestrates the flow.

CodeBuild → Builds and tests the code.

CodeDeploy/ECS/EKS → Deploys the application.

